ne injection safety\",\"categories\":[\"Medicine & infectious disease\"],\"tags\":[\"covid-19\",\"mrna vaccine\",\"myocarditis\",\"intravenous injection\",\"medical research\"],\"quote\":null,\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"nCgQDjiotG0\",\"title\":\"Google Chrome Speed Tests\",\"channel\":\"Google Chrome\",\"summary\":\"Google's 2010 Chrome ad filmed web page rendering with a high-speed camera, comparing it with a potato gun, a pirate ship and lightning. It presented browser speed as Chrome's key selling point.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it as an example of Chrome's speed-focused marketing and of how fast Chrome seemed at launch compared with other browsers.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"chrome speed marketing\",\"categories\":[\"Web development\",\"Tech company stories & documentaries\",\"Product, marketing & sales\"],\"tags\":[\"google chrome\",\"browser speed\",\"advertising\",\"high-speed camera\",\"browser wars\"],\"quote\":\"Chrome was seriously fast when it launched. It made everything else seem a decade old.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"nLv_INgaLq8\",\"title\":\"C++ Weekly - Ep 19 C++14 For The Commodore 64\",\"channel\":\"C++ Weekly With Jason Turner\",\"summary\":\"An episode of C++ Weekly in which Jason Turner shows modern C++14 compiling to the Commodore 64's 6502. It demonstrates writing efficient high-level code for 8-bit hardware.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited as an example of using modern C++ on 8-bit hardware.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"c++ on retro hardware\",\"categories\":[\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Demoscene & retrocomputing\"],\"tags\":[\"c++14\",\"commodore 64\",\"6502\",\"embedded\",\"compilers\"],\"quote\":null,\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"nNMNglk_CvE\",\"title\":\"T-Mobile’s path to IPv6 Only\",\"channel\":\"Rocky Mountain IPv6 Taskforce\",\"summary\":\"A Rocky Mountain IPv6 Taskforce talk in which T-Mobile's Stephan Lagerholm explains how the carrier went IPv6-only using DNS64 and 464XLAT, with a live Android demo and best practices.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is the standard citation in IPv6 threads that large mobile carriers already run phones with only IPv6 addresses.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"ipv6 deployment\",\"categories\":[\"Networking & internet infrastructure\"],\"tags\":[\"ipv6\",\"dns64\",\"464xlat\",\"t-mobile\",\"cgnat\"],\"quote\":\"Here's a dude from T-Mobile explaining how they have (in 2017) over ten million end-customer devices without any IPv4 addresses assigned\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"nWTvXbQHwWs\",\"title\":\"Chris Lattner: The Future of Computing and Programming Languages | Lex Fridman Podcast #131\",\"channel\":\"Lex Fridman\",\"summary\":\"Lex Fridman's long interview with Chris Lattner, creator of LLVM and Swift, covering the future of computing and programming languages. It touches on compilers, hardware, and his work at Apple, Tesla, Google and SiFive.\",\"why_hn\":\"Mentioned as an interesting Lattner conversation whenever Swift, LLVM or language design come up.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"programming languages and compilers\",\"categories\":[\"Compilers, interpreters & program analysis\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"Programming language design\"],\"tags\":[\"chris lattner\",\"swift\",\"llvm\",\"risc-v\",\"lex fridman\"],\"quote\":\"I watched a couple of Lex Fridman podcasts this week with Chris Lattner (creator of Swift) that I thought were interesting\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"n_6p-1J551Y\",\"title\":\"The Cubli: a cube that can jump up, balance, and 'walk'\",\"channel\":\"Gajamohan Mohanarajah (Gajan)\",\"summary\":\"A demo of the Cubli, a 15 cm cube that uses reaction wheels to jump up onto a corner, balance there and fall in a controlled way to move. The video shows these three abilities chained in