ing segment arguing that political motives, including Nixon's, lay behind making marijuana illegal. It mixes comedy with history.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited in cannabis and drug-war threads as an eye-opening look at the political reasons for criminalization.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"history of marijuana prohibition\",\"categories\":[\"Law, policing & justice\",\"World history\",\"Satire & parody\"],\"tags\":[\"drug war\",\"cannabis\",\"nixon\",\"adam conover\",\"history\"],\"quote\":\"What I found pretty eye-opening in the sense of \\\"Oh wow, that's so obvious\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"sXwDrcd1t-E\",\"title\":\"This ESP32 Antenna Array Can See WiFi\",\"channel\":\"Jeija\",\"summary\":\"The creator of ESPARGOS, an ESP32-based antenna array from the University of Stuttgart, explains how it senses phase and amplitude of WiFi signals to localize devices and build a WiFi camera. It demos the Python library and project.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as the companion explanation to the Espargos project, showing how phase and time differences localize devices.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"wifi sensing antenna arrays\",\"categories\":[\"Embedded systems & DIY electronics\",\"Electronics & circuit design\",\"Networking & internet infrastructure\"],\"tags\":[\"esp32\",\"phased array\",\"wifi sensing\",\"mimo\",\"localization\"],\"quote\":\"There's an excellent companion video here\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"sln-gJaURzk\",\"title\":\"Google I/O 2012 - Meet the Go Team\",\"channel\":\"Google for Developers\",\"summary\":\"A fireside Q&A with the Go team (Pike, Thompson, Griesemer and others) at Google I/O 2012. They answer audience questions about Go's design, goals, garbage collection plans and future.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people cite it for candid answers straight from Go's creators, including Ken Thompson on how the complexity of C++0x motivated Go.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"go language design\",\"categories\":[\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Programming language design\"],\"tags\":[\"go\",\"golang\",\"language design\",\"ken thompson\",\"q&a\",\"google i/o\"],\"quote\":\"If you're curious/interested in Go, it's worth watching the whole talk.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"sqIz-RNUL1g\",\"title\":\"DEFCON 20: Bigger Monster, Weaker Chains: The National Security Agency and the Constitution\",\"channel\":\"Christiaan008\",\"summary\":\"A DEFCON 20 panel with NSA whistleblower William Binney, journalist James Bamford and ACLU lawyers on the NSA's mass collection of communications and its conflict with the Constitution.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as a prescient talk that discussed, in 2012, much of what Snowden's leaks later confirmed.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"nsa surveillance\",\"categories\":[\"Privacy, surveillance & cybercrime\",\"Free speech, censorship & civil liberties\",\"Security & hacking\"],\"tags\":[\"nsa\",\"mass surveillance\",\"william binney\",\"aclu\",\"defcon\"],\"quote\":\"lot of the stuff which was later leaked by Snowden is discussed here\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"sumZLwFXJqE\",\"title\":\"MMO Economies - Hyperinflation, Reserve Currencies & You! - Extra Credits\",\"channel\":\"Extra Credits\",\"summary\":\"An Extra Credits episode on how MMO economies get overrun by inflation when players can mint unlimited gold by killing monsters. It covers approaches borrowed from real-world economics, such as anchoring or pegging currency, as alternatives to simple gold sinks.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited in game-economy threads as a clear explanation of why vendor-sellable loot causes hyperinflation and how designers counter it.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"game economy design\",\"categories\":[\"Game design\",\"Microeconomics & behavioral economics\",\"Gaming culture & analysis\"],\"tags\":[\"mmo\",\"inflation\",\"virtual currency\",\"gold sinks\",\"game design\"],\"quote\":\"Extra creditz just published an excellent video on why auto-generated vendor-sellable items lead to hyperinflation under most circumstances\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"svEuG_ekNT0\",\"title\":\"Voting Machines: Last Week Tonight with John Oliver (HBO)\",\"channel\":\"LastWeekTonight\",\"summary\":\"John Oliver's Last Week Tonight segment on US voting machines, covering how they work, their security weaknesses, and how elections could be made more secure.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited in election security threads as a watchable overview of voting machine problems.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"voting machine security\",\"categories\":[\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"US politics & elections\",\"Security & hacking\"],\"tags\":[\"voting machines\",\"election security\",\"john oliver\",\"last week tonight\"],\"quote\":\"The Last Week Tonight on voting machines was quite something to watch\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"t-_VPRCtiUg\",\"title\":\"The Surprising Secret of Synchronization\",\"channel\":\"Veritasium\",\"summary\":\"Veritasium explores how synchronization arises spontaneously in nature, with metronomes, fireflies and bridges, drawing on Steven Strogatz's work on chaos and complexity.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited as an intuitive illustration of synchronization, particularly the metronome demonstration.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"synchronization\",\"categories\":[\"Everyday physics & science demos\",\"Fundamental physics\"],\"tags\":[\"strogatz\",\"coupled oscillators\",\"metronomes\",\"complexity\",\"physics\"],\"quote\":\"it is worth watching the entire video\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"t52LB2fYhoY\",\"title\":\"Course: Zionist Editing on Wikipedia\",\"channel\":\"Israel National News - Arutz Sheva\",\"summary\":\"A short news segment from Arutz Sheva about a course on pro-Israel editing of Wikipedia.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it, mostly critically, as evidence of organized political efforts to influence Wikipedia content.\",\"content_type\":\"news\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"wikipedia editing campaigns\",\"categories\":[\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"Internet culture & memes\"],\"tags\":[\"wikipedia\",\"political editing\",\"influence operations\",\"astroturfing\"],\"quote\":null,\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"tBJ0GvsQeak\",\"title\":\"Riding in a Self Driving Car - With Nobody Up Front | JJRicks Rides With Waymo #1\",\"channel\":\"JJRicks\",\"summary\":\"JJRicks rides in Waymo's fully driverless Level 4 cars in Phoenix with nobody in the front seats, across two long rides. It is the first in a series of such ride videos.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people share it as first-hand evidence that driverless Waymo rides were already happening, including navigating a busy mall parking lot.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"waymo self-driving rides\",\"categories\":[\"Self-driving & autonomous vehicles\"],\"tags\":[\"waymo\",\"self-driving cars\",\"autonomous vehicles\",\"phoenix\",\"ride footage\"],\"quote\":\"Check out this video, it works really well\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"tCRPUv8V22o\",\"title\":\"Bytebeat: Music from very short programs - the 3rd iteration\",\"channel\":\"Ville-Matias Heikkilä (viznut)\",\"summary\":\"Viznut's third bytebeat video presents music generated by very short C programs and JavaScript expressions. It links to online tools and deeper analysis of one-line music.\",\"why_hn\":\"Linked in threads about algorithmic and demoscene music as the canonical introduction to bytebeat.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"bytebeat algorithmic music\",\"categories\":[\"Demoscene & retrocomputing\",\"Creative coding & generative art\",\"Music theory, synthesis & production\"],\"tags\":[\"bytebeat\",\"demoscene\",\"generative music\",\"bit manipulation\",\"viznut\"],\"quote\":\"Surprisingly complex music from very simple modular arithmetic and bit shifting.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"tLdRBsuvVKc\",\"title\":\"Dev Deletes Entire Production Database, Chaos Ensues\",\"channel\":\"Kevin Fang\",\"summary\":\"Kevin Fang comically retells GitLab's 2017 incident in which an engineer deleted the production database, based on the company's postmortems. It walks through the confusion and failed backups.\",\"why_hn\":\"Shared as a funny, accurate retelling of a classic outage, especially on a bad ops day.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"database outage postmortem\",\"categories\":[\"Cloud, DevOps & reliability\",\"Databases & storage\",\"Satire & parody\"],\"tags\":[\"gitlab\",\"postmortem\",\"production database\",\"devops\",\"incident\"],\"quote\":\"The reenactment by Kevin Fang is always a good source of comedy on a bad day.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0}",
    ",{\"vid\":\"tSnnfUj1XCQ\",\"title\":\"Intentional Programming demo (Part 1) - Editor\",\"channel\":\"dftube\",\"summary\":\"A roughly ten-minute demo of Microsoft's Intentional Programming research system, showing its editor and an unusual way of working with code. It is the first of two clips cut from a longer video.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters cite it as an early example of projectional or intent-based programming tools when discussing alternatives to text-based code.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"intentional programming\",\"categories\":[\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"History of computing\",\"Programming language design\"],\"tags\":[\"charles simonyi\",\"projectional editing\",\"microsoft research\",\"programming tools\"],\"quote\":\"Here are a couple of demo vids from well over 10 years ago\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"tVo_wkxH9dU\",\"title\":\"BBC: Spaghetti-Harvest in Ticino | Switzerland Tourism\",\"channel\":\"Switzerland\",\"summary\":\"The 1957 BBC Panorama April Fools' hoax report showing a Swiss family harvesting spaghetti from trees, republished by Switzerland Tourism. It is a short deadpan newsreel clip with a plummy narrator that famously fooled viewers who knew little about pasta.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN uses it mostly as a joke reference whenever pasta, harvests or April Fools pranks come up, and a few recommend it as a perfectly executed hoax.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"april fools hoaxes\",\"categories\":[\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"spaghetti tree\",\"bbc\",\"hoax\",\"april fools\",\"panorama\"],\"quote\":\"Come for the legendary and perfectly executed hoax. Stay for the plummy accent.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"tWZOEFvczzA\",\"title\":\"ANIME ROCK, PAPER, SCISSORS 2\",\"channel\":\"Corridor Digital\",\"summary\":\"Episode 2 of Corridor Digital's Anime Rock, Paper, Scissors, a short film made with AI-assisted animation techniques such as Stable Diffusion and DreamBooth. The story follows King Philip facing his twin brother and a barbarian king.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it as an example of what a very small crew can make with generative AI tools, while noting how much manual work it still takes.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"ai-assisted animation\",\"categories\":[\"LLMs & generative AI\",\"Animation & visual effects\",\"Filmmaking & video production\"],\"tags\":[\"stable diffusion\",\"anime\",\"corridor digital\",\"generative ai\",\"dreambooth\"],\"quote\":\"Corridor crew did a second video which is a lot better but still a lot of work.\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"tYytVzbPky8\",\"title\":\"HOW TO DATAMOSH: PART 1\",\"channel\":\"datamosher\",\"summary\":\"A how-to video on datamoshing, the glitch-art technique of removing keyframes so that motion data smears across video. It walks through the tools needed, such as ffmpegx and Avidemux, though the author notes parts are outdated.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited in glitch-art threads as the non-programmatic way to datamosh by deleting keyframes.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"datamoshing\",\"categories\":[\"Creative coding & generative art\",\"Audio & video engineering\"],\"tags\":[\"glitch art\",\"video compression\",\"keyframes\",\"ffmpeg\",\"avidemux\"],\"quote\":\"Check out this how-to for the non-Ruby way to do it\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"tZBViI8ZaU0\",\"title\":\"Ken Stanley: Discovery Without Objectives\",\"channel\":\"fzambetta75\",\"summary\":\"A 92-minute RMIT University seminar in which Ken Stanley argues that setting an objective can block achieving it. He presents objective-less, novelty-driven search as an alternative approach to innovation and discovery, drawing on evolutionary computation.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited as a source for the idea that open-ended search can beat objective-driven optimization, though one commenter found parts of it fluffy.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"open-ended search\",\"categories\":[\"Machine learning & deep learning\",\"Scientific method & science communication\",\"Evolution & genetics\"],\"tags\":[\"ken stanley\",\"novelty search\",\"evolutionary computation\",\"innovation\",\"objectives\"],\"quote\":\"I've seen a couple of really interesting talks recently about the surprising successfulness of objective-less search by Ken Stanley\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"ta7nlkI5K5g\",\"title\":\"Measure the mass of an eyelash with a DIY microbalance\",\"channel\":\"Applied Science\",\"summary\":\"Ben Krasnow of Applied Science builds a DIY microbalance from an analog panel meter with an optical servo loop. It reaches about 5 micrograms of precision and is used to weigh a single eyelash at around 35 micrograms.\",\"why_hn\":\"Pointed to as proof that you can weigh micrograms cheaply at home, and as a clear breakdown of how such a scale works.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"diy microbalance\",\"categories\":[\"Embedded systems & DIY electronics\",\"Electronics & circuit design\",\"3D printing, CNC & maker projects\"],\"tags\":[\"precision measurement\",\"servo loop\",\"lab equipment\",\"diy\",\"analog meter\"],\"quote\":\"YouTube just dug up a a real nice breakdown that Ben Krasnow did\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"tf1VA5jqmRo\",\"title\":\"Why is India so filthy? | The Ugly Indian | TEDxBangalore\",\"channel\":\"TEDx Talks\",\"summary\":\"A TEDxBangalore talk by The Ugly Indian, an anonymous collective, asking why Indians tolerate filthy public spaces. It describes their volunteer approach of quietly cleaning up spots and turning the tragedy of the commons into a victory.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it when discussing litter and civic neglect in India, as an example of anonymous citizen-led cleanup.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"civic cleanup in india\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\",\"Social issues & culture\",\"Environment, agriculture & food systems\"],\"tags\":[\"tragedy of the commons\",\"india\",\"urban planning\",\"volunteering\",\"tedx\"],\"quote\":\"the ugly Indian explains a bit about exactly this issue\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"tg5RFeSfBM4\",\"title\":\"October CincyRb - Jim Weirich on Decoupling from Rails\",\"channel\":\"Launch Scout\",\"summary\":\"A Cincinnati Ruby meetup talk by Jim Weirich on decoupling application logic from Rails. He argues for separating business logic from framework and database models.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited in HN debates on testing and clean architecture as a concrete example of keeping domain code separate from Rails so tests run fast.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"software architecture\",\"categories\":[\"Software architecture & design\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\"],\"tags\":[\"rails\",\"ruby\",\"decoupling\",\"unit testing\",\"clean architecture\"],\"quote\":\"was quite interesting, and argued a complete separation of business logic from the model code\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":1.0},{\"vid\":\"thnXzUFJnfQ\",\"title\":\"Amiga music: Captain - Space Debris\",\"channel\":\"Kuokka77\",\"summary\":\"A 1991 Amiga tracker module, 'Space Debris' by Captain (Markus Kaarlonen), uploaded as a video of the music. It is a well-known piece of demoscene-era computer music.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited as a classic of Amiga music, and commenters use it as a reference or comparison track.\",\"content_type\":\"music\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"amiga music\",\"categories\":[\"Demoscene & retrocomputing\",\"Music theory, synthesis & production\"],\"tags\":[\"amiga\",\"tracker music\",\"demoscene\",\"1991\",\"chiptune\"],\"quote\":\"you can't mention Amiga music wit